 "observations": [
  "The page number «146» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 35», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L02), in bold, the sigla in roman; L02, the place of Jupiter's node, in smaller bold type, «2 S. 22 G. 1 Min.», without sigla or note. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the titles in two lines, «C 60r, O 117v,» and «N 12v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 144-145 (JP2 0176-0177), with «Augmentum1) proprietatum omnium» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium», the note number after «Augmentum»; «Sublimatio ascensus Jovis examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio centri»; «Statio prima Jovis»; «Latitudo Jovis ascendentis4)», where pp. 144-145 have «accedentis», over «prima» and «secunda». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L03-L07), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L08, the units, in bold.",
  "L09-L38. Checked as on p. 144 (JP2 0176): the Sublimatio is 5s 22° 32′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 64° and 72°. At 64° (L12), 5s 18° 8′ stands for 5s 18° 6′, half of 8° 52′ being 4° 26′; the column runs 18° 9′, 18° 8′, 18° 4′ from 63° to 65°, and no note of the apparatus bears on the 8. At 72° (L20), 5s 17° 45′ answers to an Examinatio argumenti of 9° 34′ or 9° 35′, where the table has 9° 36′. The figures that Suter compares at 70° (p. 50, JP2 0082, L31), Sublimatio 167° 50′, Examinatio centri 4° 44′ and Examinatio argumenti 9° 25′, are those of L18.",
  "L09-L38. The Examinatio argumenti agrees with the equation of the epicycle of radius 11.3 of pp. 144-145 to within 2.1′ in every row: it is up to 2.1′ smaller from 61° to 84° (65°: 8° 57′ for 8° 59.1′) and from 85° to 90° between 0.3′ smaller and 1.1′ larger (90°: 10° 41′ for 10° 40.0′). The Latitudo secunda is 2° 30′ · sin x to within 0.8″ in every row, and 2° 30′ 0″, the greatest latitude, at 90°.",
  "L09-L38, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 1′ to 3′ a degree to 5° 6′ at 89° and 90°, its greatest value, the 5° 6′ of p. 144 (JP2 0176); it lies up to 3.7′ below 5° 6′ · sin x. The Statio prima increases from 4s 4° 54′ to 4s 5° 42′ by 2′, 2′ and 1′ in turn. The Latitudo prima is 1° 24′ to 66° and then decreases by 1′ every fourth degree, to 1° 18′ from 87° on.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 3s 0°, is N's; C and O have 2. Checked: the readings of C and O in notes b, c and e, where N has «xxxo» (as on p. 139, JP2 0171, N07), agree with the rules above: the Examinatio argumenti 10° 38′ at 89°, the Examinatio centri 4° 38′ at 67°, the Latitudo secunda 2° 29′ 38″ at 86°, 2° 29′ 38.1″ computed; O's 2° 21′ 50″ at 71° (note d), 2° 21′ 49.7″ computed.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01: the ellipsis «. . . .» of the lemma as printed."
 ],
